At the moment my lab network retrieves its IP's manually, which is fine at this scale but will not work on a larger network
or with a variety of machines. Adding DHCP will automatically assign IP's, subnet mask, the default gateway and a DNS server
when a client connects, eliminating the need to manually apply this information to each device.

A DHCP will need to be established AFTER the DNS and before GPO's are created.


Step 1: Authorizing DHCP server in AD

Add-DhcpServerInDC -DnsName WIN-U0PQBSD70JM.lab.TracenAcademy.internal -IPAddress 172.16.156.10
AD has to approve a DHCP server before it will give out leases and prevents unauthorized DHCP servers on the domain
(confirm with Get-DhcpServerInDC)

Step 2: Creating scope
Add-DhcpServerv4Scope -Name "Lab-Clients" -StartRange 172.16.156.100 -EndRange 172.16.156.200 -SubnetMask 255.255.255.0
Creates a pool of addresses that the DHCP can dole out, using .100 - .200 is common practice so they do not overlap with 
gateways, networking devices or the DC (.1 .2 or .10)

Step 3: Set the options the clients receive
Set-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId 172.16.156.0 -Router 172.16.156.2 -DnsServer 172.16.156.10 -DnsDomain lab.TracenAcademy.internal
Each client will get the gateway, the Domain Controller as the DNS and the domain name. The DC as the DNS is critical for 
finding the domain and applying GPOs

Step 4: Activating the scope
Set-DhcpServerv4Scope -ScopeId 172.16.156.0 -State Active

Scopes are inactive by default and wont serve leases until this activation

CONFIRMATION, CONFIRMATION, CONFIRMATION

Get-DhcpServerv4Scope
Get-DhcpServerv4OptionValue -ScopeId 172.16.156.0

